<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Bukharan People's Soviet Republic</h1><p class="st-meta">1920 CE – 1924 CE · capital Bukhara</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Soviet-backed republic established after the Red Army stormed Bukhara in 1920, led by the Young Bukharan reformer Fayzulla Khodzhayev.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In September 1920 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-frunze">Mikhail Frunze</a>, supporting a revolt by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/young-bukharans">Young Bukharans</a> and Bukharan communists, captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> and drove out the last emir. The Bukharan People's Soviet Republic was proclaimed in October 1920.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fayzulla-khodzhayev">Fayzulla Khodzhayev</a> as chairman of the council of people's nazirs, the republic carried out land and educational reforms but remained dependent on Soviet troops and money. It faced the Basmachi resistance, which in 1921 and 1922 was joined by the Ottoman general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enver-pasha">Enver Pasha</a>, killed in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> in 1922. It became the Bukharan Soviet Socialist Republic in 1924.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the national delimitation of 1924 its territory was divided between the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/uzbek-ssr">Uzbek SSR</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tajik-assr">Tajik ASSR</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/turkmen-ssr">Turkmen SSR</a>. Khodzhayev became head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a> government and was executed in 1938.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/bukharan-peoples-soviet-republic.jpg" alt="Bukharan People's Soviet Republic" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Mysid · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Bukharan_People%27s_Soviet_Republic.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1920 CE – 1924 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bukhara</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Fayzulla Khodzhayev, Abdulqodir Muhiddinov</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Persian, Uzbek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>, <a href="/countries/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a>, <a href="/countries/turkmenistan">Turkmenistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bukharan SSR, Bukharan People's Republic</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1922&amp;focus=bukharan-peoples-soviet-republic" aria-label="Bukharan People's Soviet Republic on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6268 754 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Bukharan People's Soviet Republic lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6588 1010L6588 1010M6588 1010L6691 960"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6588 1010h0M6691 960h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6588 1010h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6588 1010h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Bukhara. <a href="/atlas?year=1922&amp;focus=bukharan-peoples-soviet-republic">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
